---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/Plate.astro';
import Activity from '../../components/Activity.astro';
import activity from '../../data/cases/van-damme-activity.json';
---

<Case
  title="Van Damme"
  summary="A terminal dashboard for running many Claude Code sessions in tmux, built in Rust over five months, and retired once a better tool covered the same need."
  facts={[
    { label: 'Type', value: 'Open-source developer tool' },
    { label: 'My role', value: 'Design, development, maintenance' },
    { label: 'Timeline', value: 'Mar – Jul 2026' },
    { label: 'Stack', value: 'Rust, Ratatui, tmux, Claude Code hooks' },
    { label: 'Status', value: 'Retired at v0.9.17, replaced by Orca' },
  ]}
  links={[{ label: 'Source on GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/gvc/van-damme' }]}
>
  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="brief">
    <h2 id="brief">The problem</h2>
    <div class="case-body">
      <p>
        Once Claude Code became my daily driver, I was running several sessions at once, each with
        its own tmux session, windows, worktree and editor, and I kept losing track of which one was
        which and which one was waiting on me.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I built a manager for them and named it Van Damme, because it manages Jean-Claude
        sessions. It was also my way into Rust: I wanted to learn the language on something I would
        use every day.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="work">
    <h2 id="work">What I built</h2>
    <ol class="decisions">
      <li>
        <h3>One task, one isolated environment</h3>
        <p>
          A short form (title, directory with autocomplete, optional first prompt, model, CLI flags)
          creates a tmux session running Claude in its own git worktree or branch, with the editor
          alongside. Existing worktrees are reused, and killing a session offers to clean its worktree
          up in the background.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Live status from Claude's own hooks</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of polling, Van Damme listens to Claude Code's lifecycle hooks, so every session
          shows at a glance whether Claude is working, waiting for permission or idle. Sessions are
          grouped by directory, searchable, and survive restarts of the dashboard.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Shipped like a real tool</h3>
        <p>
          Install and uninstall commands that wire up the hooks, shell completions, a themeable colour
          system with hot reload, workspace management, and version bumps automated through GitHub Actions:
          <strong>45 feature changes</strong> on the way to v0.9.17.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Knowing when to stop</h3>
        <p>
          It's easy to keep adding features to your own tool. When I found that
          <strong>Orca</strong> does what I needed, I stopped building and switched. Herder is next on my
          list to try. Retiring your own project when something better exists is part of the job.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="plates">
    <h2 id="plates">The tool</h2>
    <div class="plates plates--wide">
      <Plate
        number={1}
        src="/cases/van-damme/session-list.svg"
        width={800}
        height={512}
        alt="Van Damme session list: active sessions with their state and directory, and keyboard shortcuts along the bottom."
        caption="Session list, from the project README."
      />
      <Plate
        number={2}
        src="/cases/van-damme/new-task.svg"
        width={800}
        height={512}
        alt="Van Damme new task form with title, directory, prompt and arguments fields."
        caption="New task form, from the project README."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="shipped">
    <h2 id="shipped">What shipped</h2>
    <div class="case-body">
      <ul class="shipped">
        <li><strong>v0.9.17</strong>, open source, used daily for my own work until I moved to Orca.</li>
        <li><strong>112 commits</strong> of mine in five months, learning Rust along the way.</li>
      </ul>
      <Activity data={activity} label="Commits per week" />
    </div>
  </section>
</Case>
